As stated in my previous thread with Walker, it's obvious that Sparkplug was conceived for an isolated SCADA application.  As you are likely finding out, and maybe to the surprise of the spec developers, SpB has grown to be much more than that, specifically being used in distributed systems, and using spBv1.0 at the Enterprise level, not just at the cell level.

The <groupID> definition is a limitation when spBv1.0 is used at the Enterprise level: it requires applications to flatten their hierarchy into a single string.  This removes the ability to leverage MQTT and wildcards subscriptions when bridging data across parts of the business.  For example, given spBv1.0/enterprise-site-area1/# and spBv1.0/enterprise-site-area2/#, if a client wants to receive all "site" data, it must know ahead of time all the different areas that exist, and explicitly call those out, rather than spBv1.0/enterprise/site/#.  Additionally, if an area is added, all clients that are tasked with knowing all about a site must wait for an engineer to manually add another subscription to an existing list.  As IIoT scales out, and more applications pop up, there will be more places to go and modify that subscription list.

@Paulo Vitor moving here to start the conversation on a bidirectional UNS.

are you envisioning commands being sent to some devices/applications in there Enterprise but no commands to the plant?

Some ways I'm thinking this can be implemented with MQTT: (1) implement an access control list in the broker restricting who can publish to what topics, (2) any device on the plant floor only publishes and never subscribes, (3) a hybrid approach where plant devices connect to a local broker to publish and subscribe, but access to that local broker is limited to only plant devices and bridging the local broker up the Enterprise is publish only.

The Cirrus Link Azure Injector module (our company selected Azure for cloud) will deliver decoded data to blob storage as avro/json using iot hub or event hub.  Our strategy is to use the topic element to determine the destination folder for the payloads
`{
  "topic": {
    "namespace": "spBv1.0",
    "edgeNodeDescriptor": "MyGroupID/MyEdgeNodeID",
    "groupId": "MyGroupID",
    "edgeNodeId": "MyEdgeNodeID",
    "deviceId": "MyDeviceID",
    "type": "DDATA"
  },
  "payload": {
    "timestamp": 1615928752428,
    "metrics": [
      {
        "name": "Whatever/Device/Tag",
        "timestamp": 1615928748107,
        "dataType": "Int32",
        "value": 4599
      },
      {
        "name": "Another/Device/Tag",
        "timestamp": 1615928748624,
        "dataType": "Int32",
        "value": 505
      },
      {
        "name": "And/So/Forth",
        "timestamp": 1615928748623,
        "dataType": "Int32",
        "value": 817
      }
    ]
  }
}`
We are looking at building our own mqtt client to do what the Injector does, just to compare cost and performance.  We have also been playing with KQL in Azure Data Explorer to query this data and it's been pretty performant so far.  Coming from a SQL background, it's been interesting the way Kusto can pivot json into columns or convert nested arrays into new rowsets (discovering the bag_unpack and mv-expand operators were some "aha!" moments for me).

