@RickBullotta you know way too well that ISA95 != Purdue model, and that ISA95 is way more than B2MML.
For any organisation looking to standardise what they call things, and how to model things, ISA95 provides a language and a maturity to the discussion and saves the company years if not decades of try-fail-repeat when it comes to modelling MES applications.
ISA95 is just a schema, not a technology, and that schema improves over time and can be applied to the technology of the day. Can you explain why you think that ISA 95 was obsolete in 1995?

@Richard Shaw @jpmoniz  if the plant is a beer bottling line, the design capacity is likely well known and fairly static across products. Even if it's something more complicated, eventually they plant manager and planning is going to want to know the capacity. Or maybe "how much extra capacity do we have?"

In either case, the original question was "where to store data that doesn't have a good home?" I think the UNS is the right place for this. Certainly beats being in a spreadsheet on Joe's desktop because we still can't get him to save his files on the shared drive. Or a random table in the ERP that only 3 people in the company know how to access.

The issue becomes the tech debt around keeping this "homeless data" organized. Every shared drive I've ever worked with is a mess and it's really hard to find the information if you don't alread know where it is. ISA95 pt2 is great for plant data but maybe we need to form a standard that goes beyond the machines to include data like customers, employees, vendors, advertisements, etc.

