@Joe Bonhage the solace message queues are amqp, and are more aligned to RabbitMQ than to kafka.
There are some good resources around that explain the difference in approach betweek Kafka and Rabbit, but it can be explained simply in terms of smart or dumb broker/client.
Kafka is a dumb broker that requires a smart client. Rabbit is a smart broker that has a dumb client

@RickBullotta 

If looking to improve MQTT, why would an MQTT broker not always keep the latest value of all topics regardless if the retain flag is set. Then the client, when subscribing, could inform the broker if it wants the latest when subscribing or just the next value when it comes in.

The broker could allow setting a threshold of payload size for when not to save the latest value?

I think the key use case for unlimited licensing are edge deployments.  Orchestration and governance happens on a central node and then the actual instances of modeling would exist on edge nodes.

But, for a lot of industrial environments, you're right.  There's either monolithic process control or many disparate PLCs with a little-to-no edge compute.  Here, servers are where apps tend to live.  Fewer, but larger site gateway instances is probably the norm.

@Richard Shaw brings up a good point on buildings or areas.  I would also include line or cells if they're large enough.  This comes up when scaling OPC Servers or SCADA systems.  There are benefits to segmenting for performance and manageability.  Manageability is a big issue.  For example, suppose one needs to lifecycle a gateway (e.g., patch, upgrade).  Is it operational optimal to plan maintenance windows for an entire plant -or- by building or by line?  As the number of assets increases, the opportunities aggregate planned downtime decreases.  It involves idling more resources and carries a higher cost.  Rather than waiting for or coordinating a big shutdown, one can lifecycle smaller groups of nodes more efficiently.

