@Andrew Camm having Kepware in the stack and serving as a gateway is what we do. What your going to find at most brownfield sites with legacy equipment is that most equipment will not support MQTT natively, and something like Kepware will be a must considering how many layer 7 protocols it does support. (Caveat, there are some layer 7 protocols Kepware doesn't support that you'll need another gateway for, i.e. SECS/GEM).

We use Kepware at the area level, then the IoT Gateway within Kepware to transmit to a site MQTT Broker. Intelligence Hub is at this node and consumes data from the raw Kepware namespace, intelligence hub transforms the data into our standard data model, and then that data is broadcasted to the UNS for that site, in addition to further up the stack (enterprise UNS, etc).

Intelligence Hub with the use of templating and pipelines can auto detect new devices added in the Kepware namespace and do the transformation without needing additional input. So there is still some work to add the device in Kepware and add its associated tags, but once that device is added to Kepware, everything above the stack takes care of onboarding, aka you don't need to configure something in kepware, and in intelligence hub, and in canary, etc.

Sparkplug B question.  Not sure where to post this one so apologies if it needs to be somewhere else.

What is good practice for defining the properties of a metric?
I was watching the sparkPlug vid on the Ignition web site and they used this example.
`
 "metrics" [{
    "name"     : "Suction Pressure",
    "engLow"   : 0.0
    "engHigh"  : 3276.5
    "engUnit"  : "PSIA"
    "timestamp": 1725611707,
    "dataType" : "Float",
    "value"    : "30.6716"    
    }
  ]
`
For me this is a clear example of a minimal property set.
However the specification defines a property set.

"A Sparkplug™ B PropertySet object is used with a metric to add custom properties to the object. 
The PropertySet is a map expressed as two arrays of equal size, one containing the keys and one containing the values."

I'm using Node-RED sparkplug node and it creates a properties object but only for DBIRTH
`
 "metrics" [{
    "name": "ad-hoc/evn/temperature",
    "timestamp": "1725611707",
