The first thing to remember is that B2MML != ISA95. B2MML is an implementation of ISA95 that specifically addresses canonical messages between MES and ERP, and as such does a number of things that are not 100% aligned to the ISA95 standard.

To your example: raw material tanks that contain HFCS-44 and have more than 1000 litres in them, and that are connected to Mixer 101 via a pipe that currently has a CIP state of "clean"

'Mixer 101' would be modelled as an equipment.
'Piping' would be a resource relationship network with resource network connections. The network connections are directional, and so have a from resource and a to resource
Material Tanks would also be modelled as equipment

So we would get Mixer101 and it's related objects like this:

getEquipment(id:"Mixer 101"){
  connectsFrom @cascade {
    properties(filter:{name:"CIPState", value:"clean"}){
        name, value
    equipment {
        id
        equipmentClass(filter:{id:{eq:"Mixing Tank"}}){
            id
        materialLot( filter: { quantity : { ge: 1000 }}){
            materialDefinition( id: "HFCS-44" ){
                id
            }
        }
    }
}

aka could I do something like "getEquipment(id:"Mixer 101"){
  connectsFrom (filter: {relationshipType: "PipeConnection"}) @cascade {
    properties(filter:{name:"CIPState", value:"clean"}){
        name, value
    equipment {
        id
        equipmentClass(filter:{id:{eq:"Mixing Tank"}}){
            id
        materialLot( filter: { quantity : { ge: 1000 }}){
            materialDefinition( id: "HFCS-44" ){
                id
            }
        }
    }
}" Looking at the spec, it looks like you can define a connection type for this?

This is sort of the wrong question. One of the minimum technical requirements for a UNS is Edge driven, ie the device tells the UNS where it belongs. The UNS is generally not a central managed thing, like PI Asset Framework. 

